[('01M1W6QR4GKX81TAERQCF62S8G', "`--uninstall-daemon` returns via `runSetupUninstall` using the `plan` built from `steps.plan(setupOpts)` (line 580), which is derived from this repo's own `setup.json` record — but the machine-wide watch plan (`loadSetupWatchPlan`) and `installedDaemonName` resolution, which the surrounding code says is 'the authority on which watcher is installed,' are only loaded afterward at lines 596/604 and are never consulted here. If another repo renamed the shared daemon (`--daemon-name`), this repo's `--uninstall-daemon` resolves the stale/default name, reports 'no watcher was installed', and exits 0 while the real watcher keeps running — permanently unreachable by the one documented removal command."), ('01M1W4ZW2B76HKF47YTTNDQE31', 'Evidence `FilePath` values in semantic records are never validated through `validateSemanticRecordPath()` which only checks file and symbol records (line 1280-1284). Evidence paths from untrusted providers could contain path traversal sequences like `../` that bypass repository containment. While evidence is secondary metadata, it flows to storage and audit logs unchecked.'), ('01M1W4ZW27VA6DYE5ZRW34C690', "When `recordSetupWatchPlan()` fails at line 722, the workspace registration from line 701 has already succeeded but the daemon plan entry isn't persisted. The installed daemon (line 737) will have the workspace registered but no tuning parameters (interval/model/effort) recorded in the watch plan, causing it to use defaults instead of the configured values."), ('01M1W4ZW24TJSTPZJF7B91X401', 'The label width limit `width - 1 - used` at line 623 doesn\'t account for the spaces added by `strings.Join(parts, " ")` at line 633. When a gauge is present, the join operation adds spacing between spinner, gauge, and label that consumes cells beyond the calculated budget, causing the line to overflow the terminal width by 1-2 cells.'), ('01M1W4ZW21H7FHEHT7JZKYF3MD', 'When `runSeed()` fails at line 278, the manifest is never reloaded (reload only happens in the else branch at line 284). The stale manifest from line 218 is then used by `historyIndexCurrent()` at line 303, causing the history index freshness check to operate on incorrect seed state. Reload manifest after seed failures in best-effort mode.'), ('01M1W4ZW1W4K1QBQEE5DZ3BDZ6', 'The check `plan.SchemaVersion != 0 &&` accepts corrupted or zero-initialized JSON with `SchemaVersion: 0` as valid, defeating the schema versioning protection. A truncated or malformed watch plan file unmarshals to zero and is silently treated as current, potentially losing workspace configuration. Remove the `!= 0` clause to reject unversioned data.'), ('01M1W4ZW0PB0MBX9SVJ2GQXP83', 'After `writeSetupBackfillState()` fails at line 943, the backfill child process spawned at line 926 continues running but its state is not recorded. On the next `setup` run, `readSetupBackfillState()` returns false (no state file), causing a second backfill to spawn and creating double token spend. The error should halt before spawning or the spawn should be deferred until after successful state write.'), ('01M1VKBDYZBG5E6NTCC0KB6JJY', '`setupComponentMark` has no case for the `"missing"` state, so it falls through to `tui.MarkPending` (documented as "not reported yet") while the caller in `renderStatusOnboardingBlock` colors the same text with `tui.PhaseSkipped`. A missing component is a terminal, already-checked fact, not a pending one, so the glyph and text color disagree for this state.'), ('01M1VKBDYTT8VECW5P4NZF9SN2', "In `liveLine()`, after the gauge is dropped for being too wide, `limit` is still floored to `liveMinLabelWidth` (20) via `if limit < liveMinLabelWidth { limit = liveMinLabelWidth }`, even when the real available width (`width-1-used`) is smaller. On terminals narrower than ~22 columns this forces the composed spinner+label past the actual terminal width, reproducing the wrap-then-stray-line failure this file's width budgeting otherwise guards against."), ('01M1VKBDYQQDMT615NPC27C49P', "When `refreshOpts.historyIndex` is true but `historyProjectionTargetExists(brainDir)` is false (no brain directory yet, e.g. first run right after `entire enable`), this branch only calls `progress.Skip(...)` and never calls `stage(brainComponentHistory, ...)`, unlike every other branch of this history-index logic. Per this file's own invariant, a component that never reports through `stage` is indistinguishable from one that was never attempted, so `status`/`doctor` will show `history=missing` for a legitimate first-run skip."), ('01M1VKBDYNBJXGQX1WAVK4V8ZS', '`recordSetupWatchPlan` (line 722) and `writeSetupRecord` (line 748) both persist `setupOpts.daemonName` into machine-wide state unconditionally, even when `planErr != nil` (e.g. `validateDaemonName` rejected it, or `os.Executable`/home-dir resolution failed) — the `planErr` check only gates the daemon install itself at the switch below. An invalid/unusable daemon name is thus recorded as if it were live, so the next `setup` run reads it back as the "previously installed" daemon and `retireRenamedDaemon` attempts to plan/retire a name that never worked.'), ('01M1VKBDYJWC7KQTAPYTTKVH7Z', 'The "two concurrent setups" check captures `concurrent_a`/`concurrent_b` exit codes but never asserts them in the final gate (only `first_status`/`second_status`/`offline_status`/`plain_status` are checked). A regression that makes concurrent `entire-brain setup` runs fail or corrupt state would still let the script print `trial-setup: OK`.'), ('01M1VKBDYEJHHHZ75M4VAGPZBE', 'Same `trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM` + `exit $status` double-fire bug as the other demo scripts: interrupting `trial-setup.sh` re-enters `cleanup` via its own `exit`, overwriting `status` with 0, so the script reports success even when killed mid-run.'), ('01M1VKBDYBNVRYDDV2GKBHT9SV', "Same `trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM` + `exit $status` double-fire bug as `demo-agent-session.sh`: an INT/TERM-triggered `cleanup` re-enters via its own `exit`, clobbering `status` with the cleanup body's own (normally 0) exit code, so a Ctrl-C'd run reports success."), ('01M1VKBDXZKGC1T7QR0FRYBP9T', "`trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM` combined with `cleanup`'s own `exit $status` causes double-firing: on SIGINT/SIGTERM, `cleanup` runs, calls `exit $status`, which re-triggers the EXIT trap and runs `cleanup` again, overwriting `status=$?` with the (typically 0) result of the `rm -rf`/fallback. Verified empirically — an interrupted run reports exit code 0 instead of 130/143, masking that the script was killed."), ('01M1VJF478QHW2V2JQF3S9W20M', 'After detecting a stray `entire-brain` process that survived the 30s grace period (`stray=$(pgrep ...)`), the script only logs it and sets `leaked=1`; it never kills the process. The subsequent `die` call fires the `EXIT` trap, which unconditionally `rm -rf`s `$trial_dir` while the detected orphan may still be alive and operating on files under that now-deleted directory.'), ('01M1VJF47555HTY4FJCP9Q2F32', '`demo-setup.sh`\'s `cleanup()` trap only retries `rm -rf "$demo_dir"` once after a 2s sleep and never checks for or waits on the detached `entire-brain` memory-worker child that `setup` spawns (unlike `trial-setup.sh`, which polls for up to 30s). The script can exit "OK" while an orphaned process keeps running against a sandbox directory that was just deleted out from under it.'), ('01M1VJF46XK0520MT6PJE8R8KK', '`Finish`\'s terminal-line truncation truncates `label + suffix` as one unit from the tail (`render.TruncateTo`), so on a label that nearly fills the terminal width, the trailing `" failed"`/`" done"` word itself gets cropped off rather than the truncation reserving space for it — a failed step can print with no visible "failed" text, leaving only the color/mark glyph to distinguish it from success.'), ('01M1VJF46TJCE8XPH5FY9W6EBS', '`runWorkspaceRefresh` (and similarly `runWorkspaceRemove`) still does an unlocked load-modify-write of `workspace.json`, unlike the newly-locked `addWorkspaceRepoLocked`. A concurrent `workspace refresh`/`remove` racing a `setup`/`workspace add` on the same workspace can load a stale manifest and overwrite it, silently dropping the repo the locked call just added — the exact race class `addWorkspaceRepoLocked` was introduced to fix, just not closed for every writer of the file.'), ('01M1VJF46NF19P6EDZGSE2HG9M', "`applySetupRecordDefaults` seeds `setupOpts.daemonName` only from this repo's own (possibly nonexistent) per-repo record, never from the machine-wide watch plan. So on a machine where a different repo previously installed the daemon under a custom `--daemon-name`, a brand-new repo's plain, flag-less `entire-brain setup` resolves the default name, sees `installedDaemonName` report the custom name as installed, and `retireRenamedDaemon` uninstalls the shared, deliberately-named watcher, replacing it with the default — an uncommanded rename triggered by an unrelated repo's ordinary setup."), ('01M1VJF46HRDV8CH1DDC5B82GE', "When `seed` succeeds but the subsequent `distill` returns `errDistillPassBusy`, `releaseWatchAgentSpend` unconditionally rewinds `LastAgentSpendAt`/`agentCalls` back to the pre-tick value, discarding the bookkeeping for the seed spend that *did* happen. This both understates `--budget` usage and lets `--distill-every`'s interval restart early, bypassing the documented spend caps the watcher is supposed to enforce."), ('01M1VJF46E2H2A6WSB89Q7E4GJ', "`runDistill`'s non-JSON branch only prints the summary counts and never surfaces `source.Warnings`, so a `--max-sessions`-capped run (used by the detached `setup` backfill, which runs without `--json`) silently drops the one warning that says the pass stopped short of the full corpus — the log looks like a complete run when sessions were deferred."), ('01M1VJF45VWN1JQ5PET37VCCX4', '`--newest-first` (used by the production backfill/watch path) processes sessions most-recent-first while `byBranch[branch]` accumulates the running "existing" fact set in visitation order, but `reconcilePrompt()` still tells the agent "the candidate is newer/correct" unconditionally. In newest-first mode, once the newest session\'s facts are in `byBranch`, an older session\'s candidate is compared against them while still being told it\'s the newer one, so the agent can `supersede` a correct, newer fact with a stale, older candidate. `factmerge.ApplyActions` then marks the newer fact `StatusSuperseded` with nothing checking actual timestamps.')]

