s via its own `exit`, clobbering `status` with the cleanup body's own (normally 0) exit code, so a Ctrl-C'd run reports success."), ('01M1VKBDXZKGC1T7QR0FRYBP9T', "`trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM` combined with `cleanup`'s own `exit $status` causes double-firing: on SIGINT/SIGTERM, `cleanup` runs, calls `exit $status`, which re-triggers the EXIT trap and runs `cleanup` again, overwriting `status=$?` with the (typically 0) result of the `rm -rf`/fallback. Verified empirically — an interrupted run reports exit code 0 instead of 130/143, masking that the script was killed."), ('01M1VJF478QHW2V2JQF3S9W20M', 'After detecting a stray `entire-brain` process that survived the 30s grace period (`stray=$(pgrep ...)`), the script only logs it and sets `leaked=1`; it never kills the process. The subsequent `die` call fires the `EXIT` trap, which unconditionally `rm -rf`s `$trial_dir` while the detected orphan may still be alive and operating on files under that now-deleted directory.'), ('01M1VJF47555HTY4FJCP9Q2F32', '`demo-setup.sh`\'s `cleanup()` trap only retries `rm -rf "$demo_dir"` once after a 2s sleep and never checks for or waits on the detached `entire-brain` memory-worker child that `setup` spawns (unlike `trial-setup.sh`, which polls for up to 30s). The script can exit "OK" while an orphaned process keeps running against a sandbox directory that was just deleted out from under it.'), ('01M1VJF46XK0520MT6PJE8R8KK', '`Finish`\'s terminal-line truncation truncates `label + suffix` as one unit from the tail (`render.TruncateTo`), so on a label that nearly fills the terminal width, the trailing `" failed"`/`" done"` word itself gets cropped off rather than the truncation reserving space for it — a failed step can print with no visible "failed" text, leaving only the color/mark glyph to distinguish it from success.'), ('01M1VJF46TJCE8XPH5FY9W6EBS', '`runWorkspaceRefresh` (and similarly `runWorkspaceRemove`) still does an unlocked load-modify-write of `workspace.json`, unlike the newly-locked `addWorkspaceRepoLocked`. A concurrent `workspace refresh`/`remove` racing a `setup`/`workspace add` on the same workspace can load a stale manifest and overwrite it, silently dropping the repo the locked call just added — the exact race class `addWorkspaceRepoLocked` was introduced to fix, just not closed for every writer of the file.'), ('01M1VJF46NF19P6EDZGSE2HG9M', "`applySetupRecordDefaults` seeds `setupOpts.daemonName` only from this repo's own (possibly nonexistent) per-repo record, never from the machine-wide watch plan. So on a machine where a different repo previously installed the daemon under a custom `--daemon-name`, a brand-new repo's plain, flag-less `entire-brain setup` resolves the default name, sees `installedDaemonName` report the custom name as installed, and `retireRenamedDaemon` uninstalls the shared, deliberately-named watcher, replacing it with the default — an uncommanded rename triggered by an unrelated repo's ordinary setup."), ('01M1VJF46HRDV8CH1DDC5B82GE', "When `seed` succeeds but the subsequent `distill` returns `errDistillPassBusy`, `releaseWatchAgentSpend` unconditionally rewinds `LastAgentSpendAt`/`agentCalls` back to the pre-tick value, discarding the bookkeeping for the seed spend that *did* happen. This both understates `--budget` usage and lets `--distill-every`'s interval restart early, bypassing the documented spend caps the watcher is supposed to enforce."), ('01M1VJF46E2H2A6WSB89Q7E4GJ', "`runDistill`'s non-JSON branch only prints the summary counts and never surfaces `source.Warnings`, so a `--max-sessions`-capped run (used by the detached `setup` backfill, which runs without `--json`) silently drops the one warning that says the pass stopped short of the full corpus — the log looks like a complete run when sessions were deferred."), ('01M1VJF45VWN1JQ5PET37VCCX4', '`--newest-first` (used by the production backfill/watch path) processes sessions most-recent-first while `byBranch[branch]` accumulates the running "existing" fact set in visitation order, but `reconcilePrompt()` still tells the agent "the candidate is newer/correct" unconditionally. In newest-first mode, once the newest session\'s facts are in `byBranch`, an older session\'s candidate is compared against them while still being told it\'s the newer one, so the agent can `supersede` a correct, newer fact with a stale, older candidate. `factmerge.ApplyActions` then marks the newer fact `StatusSuperseded` with nothing checking actual timestamps.')]

